import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.List;

public class Solution {
    public List<List<Integer>> permute(int[] nums) {
        List<List<Integer>> results = new ArrayList<>();
        Arrays.sort(nums);
        backtrack(results, new ArrayList<>(), nums);
        return results;
    }

    private void backtrack(List<List<Integer>> results, List<Integer> current, int[] nums) {
        if (current.size() == nums.length) {
            results.add(new ArrayList<>(current));
            return;
        }
        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
            if (current.contains(nums[i])) {
                continue;
            }
            current.add(nums[i]);
            backtrack(results, current, nums);
            current.remove(current.size() - 1);
        }
    }
}
```

This function implements the backtracking algorithm to generate all possible permutations of the input array. It recursively builds permutations by adding elements to the current list and backtracking when the list reaches the required length.

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.List;

public class Solution {
    public List<List<Integer>> permute(int[] nums) {
        List<List<Integer>> result = new ArrayList<>();
        if (nums.length == 0) {
            return result;
        }
        permuteHelper(Integer.toString(nums.length), new ArrayList<Integer>(), nums, result);
        return result;
    }

    private void permuteHelper(String remaining, List<Integer> current, int[] nums, List<List<Integer>> result) {
        if (remaining.isEmpty()) {
            result.add(new ArrayList<>(current));
            return;
        }
        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
            if (remaining.charAt(0) == nums[i]) {
                current.add(nums[i]);
                permuteHelper(remaining.substring(1), current, nums, result);
                current.remove(current.size() - 1);
            }
        }
    }
}
```
Explanation:

The `permute` function initializes an empty result list and checks if the input array `nums` is empty. If it is, it returns the empty result list. Otherwise, it calls the `permuteHelper` function with the initial remaining string representation of the array length (which is empty at this point), an empty `current` list, the input array `nums`, and the result list.

The `permuteHelper` function takes a remaining string representation of the array length, the current permutation, the input array `nums`, and the result list. It checks if the remaining string is empty. If it is, it means we have processed all the elements in the array, so we add a copy of the current permutation to the result list and return.

If the remaining string is not empty, it iterates over each element in the input array and checks if the first character of the remaining string matches the current element. If it matches, it adds the current element to the `current` list, removes the first character from the remaining string, and recursively calls itself with the updated remaining string and `current` list.

This approach generates all possible permutations of the input array by exploring all possible combinations of elements in the array.

