public boolean canArrange(int[] arr, int k) {
    int[] counts = new int[k];
    for (int num : arr) {
        counts[num % k]++;
    }
    for (int i = 0; i <= k / 2; i++) {
        if (i != 0 && i != k - i || counts[i] > counts[k - i]) {
            return false;
        }
    }
    return true;
}
```
In the solution, we first calculate the frequencies of each remainder when the elements of the array are divided by `k`. Then, we check if the total count of non-zero remainders (excluding number 0 and `k/2` if `k` is even) is even. If so, we have at least one pair of the same remainder, and the rest of the remainders can be matched to form the required pairs. If the count of any non-zero remainder is odd, it is not possible to form the required pairs.

